      "Not the roses climbing the trellis, not the charming wraparound porch, not even the way the late afternoon light made the whole cottage glow like a magazine spread titled “Starting Over: Divorced and Delusional.” My eyes went straight to the split wood, the water damage from that gutter hanging by a single bracket. I could already see the fix: a new board, wood filler, maybe reinforce the railing while I was at it. Two hours, tops. Three if the rot went deeper than it looked.",
      "My hand was already reaching for the sticky notes in my purse.",
      "This is what I do, what I’ve always done. See a problem, fix the problem. It’s the only thing I’ve ever been good at and the only thing that ever made me worth keeping around. David had said so himself, right before he left: <em>You’re so busy fixing everything else, you never noticed we were broken.</em>",
      "Joke was on him. I <em>had</em> noticed. I just couldn’t figure out how to fix it.",
      "“You’ve been staring at that porch for four minutes,” my mother said from the passenger seat. “It’s a house, Gemma. Not a crime scene.”",
      "“The step is broken.”",
      "“So fix it later.”",
      "“It’s a safety hazard.”",
      "“It’s been a safety hazard for six months. The listing said ‘character and potential.’ That’s real estate code for ‘falling apart.’ You knew what you were buying.”",
      "She wasn’t wrong. I knew exactly what I was getting—a 1920s cottage that sat empty since the previous owner died, priced forty thousand under market because the estate just wanted it gone. Three bedrooms, one and a half baths, original hardwood that probably needed refinishing, a kitchen that hadn’t seen an update since the Reagan administration, and approximately nine hundred things requiring immediate attention.",
